hi there steve kaufman here hi all you  language learners remember if you enjoy  these videos please subscribe to my  channel  today i want to talk about something  that many of you are probably aware of  but i want to kind of share my views on  this the influence of the internet on  language learning and how it has already  dramatically changed language learning  and how it's going to continue to change  language learning and what triggered  this was an exchange on my blog the  linguist language  where i mentioned the fact that um in  universities i was told by the head of a  language department here at the  university of british columbia in  vancouver that there's a very  rapid drop-off in students  who enroll in in particular in difficult  languages  that after one or two years they uh you  know the enrollment drops to a fraction  of what it was for the first two years  so by the third or fourth year there are  very few people in language class which  presents a real problem for universities  it's difficult for them to justify these  classes and so forth and so on  and i said that maybe and then i also  pointed out the fact that in these  difficult languages in many cases for  example in russian even in ivy league  schools in the third or fourth year they  are reading russian literature in  translation not in the original  and so i suggested maybe the focus  should be on comprehension rather than  trying to produce the language which  doesn't seem to do much good for most of  the students anyway so someone said well  yeah but if you push people to read  literature they're going to be even less  interested and of course that is true  and and i think this gets back to one of  krashen's points and that is successful  language learning relies on compelling  input and if you force students to read  you know the canons of russian  literature dostoyevsky or whatever it  might be that's fine if the student is  interested in dostoevsky but if the  student is not interested then that's  actually counterproductive  so then i thought to myself well in  today's day and age  really why would we force the students  to learn or to read specific  content matter  uh  so i looked at what the internet offers  today first of all there's a tremendous  range of content it used to be if you  went to a language class the teacher was  the only source  of  information on how the language works  the only source of potential reading  material or listening material whereas  today any successful language learner  has to be searching for these things  himself or herself  so  i would see i mean if i look here you  know i've got my uh computer my macbook  i've got my little ipod nano  and i've got my ipad and of course i'm  using my iphone which i also use for  language learning but i'm using it to  film the uh the video so these are the  tools that we have so uh  you know if we had a language class  based on how the internet  makes things more accessible to everyone  where the teacher is no longer the  source of wisdom and content but simply  a guide  so if i were running a class i would say  let's say whatever the language is i  would say  initially day one here are some of the  issues in the language in japanese they  have these particles whatever they are  called you know  so these are some of the issues you're  going to encounter in the language or if  it's a if it's a romance language that  the verbs change for person for tense uh  and so forth and so on  and so then i would say now here's a  list of beginner material that you can  read and listen to and i would quite  frankly direct them to link because  there they can look the words up and  they can say phrases  go off and struggle through these and  then we'll come back into class  and  you bring in the phrases which they can  save and we'll go over some of the  phrases so i explained in general terms  what to expect in the language they then  go to discover the language find that  they can't understand and even if they  look up the words they can't make sense  of it so we discuss some of these  phrases in class and then send them back  to do more  reading and listening so this is sort of  in the initial period where the content  they would use would  basically be limited to short easy  material and then at a later stage  i would encourage students to go and  find whatever is of interest to them and  suggest here are some potential sources  so if you're interested in history and  politics and music and art in literature  whatever it is go off and find it and  then you know i would incur we can go  over certain aspects of the grammar in  class but of course these things don't  necessarily stick and so then i would  encourage people if you want to know how  to conjugate a certain verb if you want  to  get some more background on the use of  particles in japanese  google for it and then if you have any  questions bring them to class so that  the teacher would be  encouraging the students to find  information about the language wherever  they can find it and we can discuss it  in class encouraging to find content of  interest and then read and listen to it  on your own and bring phrases that cause  you difficulty to class  and and rather than having everyone read  aloud in class oh and i would even  encourage them to say all right in the  classroom there's 20 of us so we can't  all be speaking together sign up at  italki or at link or wherever you want  and get on with a native speaker and  then because the the tutor  typically provides a list of phrases  that cause you difficulty in the  conversation bring those to class and  we'll discuss them and if there are uh  patterns in the language that cause  difficulty for learner a typically they  also cause difficulty for other learners  so these can be discussed in the  classroom so i i mean that's just a very  quick sketch of a subject that needs to  be researched but surely the fact that  there is so much help available online  help in terms of online native speakers  help in terms of interesting content  help in terms of explanations of the  grammar and so forth and so on that the  role of the teacher as the sort of  source of all wisdom about the language  is  obsolete  and so uh getting back to my discussion  at my blog this commenter said you know  how can you have a situation where  people read what they want  doesn't everyone have to be reading the  same book in the classroom well that's  the way it's been up until now but i can  foresee a situation where people bring  whatever they've been reading to class  whatever problems they've been reading  the class and this becomes a subject of  discussion in the classroom initially  using  the language of the learner and  gradually moving into  more and more using the target language  so that's just to kind of get a  discussion going on how the internet has  changed language learning and i look  forward to your comments bye for now  you
 

